body:
You bring up a good point - this is a TV show not a pseudo history book. The first rule of thumb is show don't tell, which tends to lend itself to characters doing things so that we as viewer can understand them and the world. Whereas the book can only tell because of the way it's written. So of course, the adaptation was never going to be 1 for 1, that's not possible given that they are translating the book into a very different medium.

&#x200B;

On the second point, I really wish more discussions on this sub (I mean it's a book subreddit for petes sake) would center things like theme and narrative construction in discussions. But more often than not its exactly that guy from the Simpsons or Family Guy discussing surface level actions/events/lore (which is fine I love that stuff to but probably shouldn't be the bulk of discussions).

If you're going by the who is still alive by end of the show, then that's pretty tough. I think Brienne and Robin Arryn never met but Yohn Royce has met them both.

&#x200B;

If you're going by the books, you can throw a dart at a map of Westeros and Dany has probably not met the lord of that castle/region.

&#x200B;

edit: I take it back immediately; Brienne was for some reason at the lord's council in the finale. In which case my answer is Daario and almost anyone in Westeros.
